-- title:
--   Lebesgue differentiation along shrinking cubes on the product space
-- statement:
--   Let $\Phi : \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be locally integrable for Lebesgue measure on the product, and for $h > 0$ and $u \in \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ write $Q_h(u) = $ `closedCube_two h u` for the closed product cube of scale $h$ based at $u$ (the product of the two closed cubes of scale $h$, the sets along which the tiling of $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ shrinks).
--
--   Then for almost every $p \in \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ the following holds: for every index type $\iota$, every filter $l$ on $\iota$, and every pair of families $h_\bullet : \iota \to \mathbb{R}$ and $u_\bullet : \iota \to \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ such that
--
--   * $h_i > 0$ for every $i$,
--   * $h_i \to 0$ along $l$, and
--   * $p \in Q_{h_i}(u_i)$ for every $i$,
--
--   one has
--
--   $$\lim_{l} \; \frac{1}{|Q_{h_i}(u_i)|}\int_{Q_{h_i}(u_i)} \Phi(q)\,dq \;=\; \Phi(p).$$
--
--   Here $\frac{1}{|A|}\int_A \Phi$, written `⨍` in Lean, is the mean value of $\Phi$ over $A$, with $|A|$ the Lebesgue measure of $A$.
--
--   This is the Lebesgue differentiation theorem in the form the limiting argument needs: the cubes are *not* centred at $p$, only required to contain it, and their scale is indexed by an arbitrary filter rather than by a sequence. It is what makes the tile average of a function of a *pair* of points converge almost everywhere to that function, which is one of the two properties required of a moving dominant in the generalized dominated convergence theorem.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The quantifiers over the index type $\iota$, the filter $l$ and the two families sit *inside* the almost-everywhere statement, so a single null set works simultaneously for all of them. The index type is restricted to `Type` (universe $0$). The product $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ carries the max norm rather than the Euclidean norm of $\mathbb{R}^{2d}$, which is why the balls of the product are literally products of balls and the statement is not a transport of the $\mathbb{R}^{2d}$ result.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/LebesgueDiff2.lean#L141-L153

